class AFGCharacterPlayer;
class AFGRainAudioSubsystem;

/**
 * RAIN ON THE HUD, INDOORS — and the first thing to know is that this is NOT the rain-occlusion fix
 * with a gap in it. They are two systems, and one cannot reach the other.
 *
 * Board `m508019304` / `m6249395`. Ant reported it again this week: rain draws on screen while she
 * stands under her own foundations.
 *
 * ★ WHY EXTENDING THE OCCLUSION FIX WOULD HAVE CHANGED NOTHING. Receipts from the UNCOOKED master
 * materials, because cooking strips the expression graph that holds parameter names:
 *
 *   `MM_Rain.uasset`          references `RT_WeatherOcclusion` AND `FactoryBias`. World rain samples
 *                             the DYNAMIC buildable occlusion RT, which is what
 *                             `AFGBuildable::mRainOcclusionBoundingBox` feeds. `FFPMRainOcclusionFix`
 *                             reaches this, correctly.
 *   `PP_VisorDroplets.uasset` references NEITHER. Its only occlusion inputs are the BAKED per-cell
 *                             textures, captured `#if WITH_EDITOR` before any player placed anything.
 *
 * So the screen-space droplets are **blind to player buildables by construction**. Anyone meaning to
 * "extend the occlusion fix to cover the HUD" should stop: it would change nothing, which is the most
 * expensive kind of fix.
 *
 * ★ THE ONLY GATE IS ONE BOOLEAN, OUTSIDE THE MATERIAL:
 *
 *   `AFGRainAudioSubsystem::UpdatePlayerUnderRainState()`   (FGRainAudioSubsystem.h:79)
 *     broadcasts `OnPlayerUnderRainStateUpdated(bool)`      (:20, :42 — public, dynamic multicast)
 *       the shipped blueprint writes `bIsUnderRain`         (FGCharacterPlayer.h — public UPROPERTY)
 *         [game DLL] drives the visor material's alpha
 *
 * The rain-AUDIO subsystem has no relationship to `URainOcclusionWorldSubsystem`. It thinks the player
 * is under rain while they stand under a roof, so the post-process runs. That is the bug.
 *
 * ⚠ THE LAST ARROW IS A HYPOTHESIS. `FGCharacterPlayer.cpp` is a link stub, so the step from the bool
 * to the pixels cannot be read. **So this is built so that being wrong costs nothing:** it only writes
 * the value the game would itself hold had it noticed the roof, and hands the game's own value back
 * when the player steps out. If the DLL ignores the bool this is inert — and the log line at the first
 * suppression is what separates "the gate ran and the droplets stayed" from "the gate never ran".
 *
 * ★ IT OWNS NO ENCLOSURE TEST. It asks `FPMEnclosure`, which is the one shared check
 * (Ant: *"one check for all \"inside\" stuff"*). It registers as an `Overhead` consumer, because a roof
 * is enough for rain — a sealed room is not required, and demanding one would leave droplets running
 * under an open-sided canopy.
 *
 * NO HOOKS. A ticker, a public UPROPERTY, and a Blueprint-exposed delegate. Nothing is jump-patched.
 * NO AccessTransformers entry — both members are public as shipped, stated so nobody adds one.
 * ZERO RESIDUE — the only write is a transient property on a pawn, restored by `Disarm()`.
 */

/**
 * The delegate sink: it remembers what the GAME thinks, so the gate knows what to give back.
 *
 * Must be a UObject with a UFUNCTION — `OnPlayerUnderRainStateUpdated` is a DYNAMIC multicast
 * (`FGRainAudioSubsystem.h:20`), and dynamic delegates bind by reflected name.
 *
 * ⚠ NOT OPTIONAL, and the reason is one common case: **rain ENDS while the player is indoors.** The
 * game writes false onto a bool the gate already forced to false, so the write is invisible — "the
 * game agrees" and "the game changed its mind" look identical. Without this sink the shadow stays
 * stale at true, and stepping outside into clear weather switches the droplets ON. Worse than the bug.
 */
UCLASS()
class UFPMRainVisorSignal : public UObject
{
	GENERATED_BODY()

public:
	/** Bound to the rain-audio subsystem. Records only; never writes back. */
	UFUNCTION()
	void OnPlayerUnderRainStateUpdated(bool NewState);

	bool IsUnderRain() const { return bUnderRain; }

	/** Used while hands-off, when the character's own bool IS the game's answer. */
	void Observe(bool bValue) { bUnderRain = bValue; }

private:
	bool bUnderRain = false;
};

class FFPMRainVisorGate final : public IFPMFix
{
public:
	static FFPMRainVisorGate& Get();

	virtual const TCHAR* Name() const override { return TEXT("rain-visor-gate"); }

	/** A dedicated server has no camera and no post-process chain to draw droplets on. */
	virtual EFPMFixSide Side() const override { return EFPMFixSide::NeverOnDedicatedServer; }

	/**
	 * ChokePointRepair, and the distinction matters. The CAUSE is named with receipts — the rain-audio
	 * subsystem does not account for player buildables. But that is not where this repairs:
	 * `UpdatePlayerUnderRainState` is a link stub and `bIsPlayerUnderRain` is protected. The repair
	 * happens at the nearest reachable point downstream, which is this value and not `OriginNamed`.
	 */
	virtual EFPMOriginStatus OriginStatus() const override { return EFPMOriginStatus::ChokePointRepair; }

	virtual FPMDiag::EChannel Channel() const override { return FPMDiag::EChannel::RainVisor; }

	virtual void Arm() override;
	virtual void Disarm() override;

	/** `FPM.RainVisor.Report` — what the gate is doing and why. */
	static void ReportNow();
};
